tortoise-wow: an object's timed events free their Lua function references when its processor is removed (the object left its map or was destroyed); they stayed in the registry for the state's lifetime.

EventMgr::CleanupObjectProcessors (and the replace path of patch 0001) set
the processor's events to LUAEVENT_STATE_ERASE before destroying it.
ElunaEventProcessor::RemoveEvent skips luaL_unref for ERASE, which is meant
for a closed or reloaded state. The state here is alive, so every timer still
pending on a despawned, logged out or teleported object kept its function, and
whatever its upvalues reach, in LUA_REGISTRYINDEX. ABORT unrefs them.

A reload still marks every event ERASE (its references belong to the closed
state). SetAllEventStates now also reaches the processors parked in
replacedObjectProcessors by patch 0001, which are not in `processors`: with
ABORT they would otherwise unref the old state's reference numbers in the new
state at the next cleanup, freeing live references of the new state.

Base: ElunaLuaEngine/Eluna 1b06f28ff3a00054d915d824c725fb4283fee74d (branch
cmangos-spell-update), the dep/Eluna pin, with patches 0001-0005 applied. Eluna is
GPL-3.0 (https://github.com/ElunaLuaEngine/Eluna, LICENSE); this change is under
the same licence. Applied by cmake/ElunaSources.cmake to a copy in the build tree.
Candidate to upstream to Eluna (Eluna master, last changed in ElunaEventMgr.cpp by a80c638, has the same ERASE in CleanupObjectProcessors).
diff --git a/ElunaEventMgr.cpp b/ElunaEventMgr.cpp
index 9fe50d4..ec2af49 100644
--- a/ElunaEventMgr.cpp
+++ b/ElunaEventMgr.cpp
@@ -210,6 +210,13 @@ void EventMgr::SetAllEventStates(LuaEventState state)
 {
     for (auto* processor : processors)
         processor->SetStates(state);
+
+    // Replaced processors are no longer in `processors` but are destroyed
+    // only at the next cleanup. A reload (ERASE) must reach their events too:
+    // their references belong to the state being closed, and unreferencing
+    // them in the new state would free its live references.
+    for (auto& processor : replacedObjectProcessors)
+        processor->SetStates(state);
 }
 
 void EventMgr::SetEventState(int eventId, LuaEventState state)
@@ -241,8 +248,10 @@ uint64 EventMgr::CreateObjectProcessor(WorldObject* obj)
         // Flagged for deletion but not cleaned up yet. emplace below would
         // keep the old entry and destroy the new processor while `processors`
         // still held its pointer (use after free in UpdateProcessors).
+        // ABORT, not ERASE: the state is alive, so the old processor's
+        // events free their Lua function references when it is destroyed.
         ElunaEventProcessor* old = existing->second.get();
-        old->SetStates(LUAEVENT_STATE_ERASE);
+        old->SetStates(LUAEVENT_STATE_ABORT);
         processors.erase(old);
         objectProcessorsPendingDelete.erase(id);
         replacedObjectProcessors.push_back(std::move(existing->second));
@@ -287,8 +296,14 @@ void EventMgr::CleanupObjectProcessors()
         if (it == objectProcessors.end())
             continue;
 
+        // ABORT, not ERASE: ERASE keeps the event's Lua function reference
+        // (it is for a closed or reloaded state, whose references are gone).
+        // This state is alive, so the processor's destructor must unref every
+        // pending event, or each object that leaves its map or is destroyed
+        // with a timer pending leaves its function (and everything the
+        // function's upvalues reach) in the registry for the state's lifetime.
         ElunaEventProcessor* p = it->second.get();
-        p->SetStates(LUAEVENT_STATE_ERASE);
+        p->SetStates(LUAEVENT_STATE_ABORT);
 
         processors.erase(p);
         objectProcessors.erase(it);
